-- | Ipe.Secret.Unsafe — the raw secret-reveal escape hatch for `Ipe.Secret`
--.
--
-- This submodule is the one home for the member that un-seals a `Secret` back
-- into a bare `String`. Importing it discloses the `unsafe` capability
-- program-wide, so a dependency's raw secret-reveal is visible before the
-- program runs.
--
-- `unsafeReveal` is a point-free `Kernel.kernel "Secret_reveal"` alias to the
-- retained `SecretReveal` kernel (runtime: `ipe_runtime::secret::secret_reveal`,
-- the single greppable un-parse) — the SAME kernel and the SAME sealed-newtype
-- barrier as before; only the surface home moved here from `Ipe.Secret`. The
-- typed-secret barrier (redaction, no-`Debug` leak, `zeroize`-on-`Drop`,
-- constant-time compare) is unchanged.
--
-- The safe defaults stay on the native `Ipe.Secret` surface: `Secret.use`
-- (apply a function to the plaintext inside a scope and return a non-secret
-- result) covers the common case WITHOUT disclosing `unsafe`. Reach for
-- `unsafeReveal` only for the residual cases a scoped consume cannot express.
module Ipe.Secret.Unsafe exposing (unsafeReveal)


import Ipe.Ffi.Kernel as Kernel


-- | `unsafeReveal s` — un-seal a `Secret` into its raw plaintext `String`. The
-- `unsafe` prefix names the secret-leak risk at every call site: the returned
-- plaintext escapes the sealed newtype's protections (redaction, no-`Debug`,
-- zeroize-on-drop), and the caller owns where it lands. Prefer the scoped
-- `Secret.use` — it keeps the plaintext inside a closure and stays off the
-- `unsafe` axis; use `unsafeReveal` only where a scoped consume cannot express
-- the need.
unsafeReveal : Secret -> String
unsafeReveal = Kernel.kernel "Secret_reveal"
